    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. Could you get a bit more specific on the \"wear and tear\" part? Specifically, I'm worried about how these surfaces handle heavy foot traffic—like scuffs from hard-soled shoes or small bits of grit/debris getting ground into the surface over time. Which one is most likely to look \"beaten up\" after a few years?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "When considering heavy foot traffic and abrasive grit, the durability rankings shift slightly, though it depends on the quality of the installation. Polished concrete, when properly sealed and finished, handles grit surprisingly well; the inherent hardness resists deep scratching, but persistent grit can dull the shine over time, making it look less pristine. Natural stone is also very resilient, but if it's a softer variety, the grit can cause micro-abrasions that etch the surface, leading to a duller appearance around high-traffic zones. LVT, while excellent for daily maintenance, is often the most susceptible to becoming \"beaten up\" visually from constant, heavy abrasion, especially if the wear layer is thin.",
      "source": "target",
